WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:03.537
- I always knew as a
kid that I was a writer,

00:00:03.537 --> 00:00:05.399
but I never really knew
what I wanted to do.

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:07.303
Whenever you see me somewhere succeeding

00:00:07.303 --> 00:00:10.977
in one area of my life,
that almost certainly means

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:13.788
I am failing in another area of my life.

00:00:13.788 --> 00:00:16.296
Literally not allowed to
play Scrabble in my house

00:00:16.296 --> 00:00:17.531
(audience laughs)

00:00:17.531 --> 00:00:19.451
because of fights that break out.

00:00:19.451 --> 00:00:20.859
I tried to write a novel,

00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:23.426
like a really intricate
Toni Morrison-esque novel,

00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:25.155
and it is terrible.

00:00:25.155 --> 00:00:27.365
- She's a television producer and writer.

00:00:27.365 --> 00:00:30.105
She's a creator, head writer,
and executive producer

00:00:30.105 --> 00:00:33.106
of the hit medical drama
series Grey's Anatomy.

00:00:33.106 --> 00:00:35.893
She was named one of Time
Magazine's 100 People

00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:37.521
Who Helped Shape the World.

00:00:37.521 --> 00:00:38.510
She's Shonda Rhimes,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.307
and here's my take on her
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.053
Rule number 10 is my personal favorite,

00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:44.564
and I'm curious to figure out which one

00:00:44.564 --> 00:00:45.916
you guys like the most.

00:00:45.916 --> 00:00:47.945
Also, as Shonda's talking,
if she says something

00:00:47.945 --> 00:00:50.142
that really, really
deeply resonates with you,

00:00:50.142 --> 00:00:51.871
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:51.871 --> 00:00:52.944
and put quotes around it

00:00:52.944 --> 00:00:55.274
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:55.274 --> 00:00:58.027
(whooshing)

00:00:58.027 --> 00:01:00.694
(soaring music)

00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:09.263
- I spent a lot a time,

00:01:09.263 --> 00:01:10.516
and a lot of people spend a lot a time,

00:01:10.516 --> 00:01:12.243
thinking of all the things they want to do

00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:13.602
and talking about it.

00:01:13.602 --> 00:01:16.104
But the idea that you're
not actually doing anything

00:01:16.104 --> 00:01:17.345
to get to where you want to go,

00:01:17.345 --> 00:01:19.720
you're waiting for some
sort of big magical moment

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.899
or some door to open
or some, I don't know,

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:26.345
some interesting,
illuminating thing to happen

00:01:26.345 --> 00:01:29.191
is very different from the hard day-to-day

00:01:29.191 --> 00:01:32.195
slogging through step by step,
I'm going to climb this ladder,

00:01:32.195 --> 00:01:34.513
here's an opportunity,
I'm going to run for it,

00:01:34.513 --> 00:01:36.313
any little small moment.

00:01:36.313 --> 00:01:37.810
Oh, I want to be a writer.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.963
Somebody wants me to be an intern

00:01:38.963 --> 00:01:39.928
at their production company.

00:01:39.928 --> 00:01:40.920
I will go do that.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.026
Somebody wants me to go work at this job,

00:01:43.026 --> 00:01:44.090
I will do that.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.400
Oh, I'm going to go work
at this sort of awful job

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.387
because it's going to pay the bills

00:01:47.387 --> 00:01:49.100
so I can write at night,
well, I'm going to do that

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.880
because that's a way to
get where I want to go.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:52.849
People don't want to do those things

00:01:52.849 --> 00:01:55.188
because they think that they're
waiting for their big break,

00:01:55.188 --> 00:01:57.547
and I think that hard
work and being a doer

00:01:57.547 --> 00:01:59.542
and taking every opportunity
that comes your way,

00:01:59.542 --> 00:02:03.826
whether or not it seems
like the most amazing one

00:02:03.826 --> 00:02:04.825
is the way to go.

00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:08.034
I always knew as a kid
that I was a writer,

00:02:08.034 --> 00:02:09.931
but I never really knew
what I wanted to do.

00:02:09.931 --> 00:02:12.665
I always believed that
that wasn't a real job.

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.856
So I thought that all of the
things that interested me,

00:02:15.856 --> 00:02:19.376
like maybe I'd be a doctor,
maybe I'd be a lawyer,

00:02:19.376 --> 00:02:20.830
maybe I'd go work in Washington.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.211
I always thought that
those were the things

00:02:22.211 --> 00:02:24.686
that I actually wanted to do for a living

00:02:24.686 --> 00:02:26.113
instead of understanding
that those were the things

00:02:26.113 --> 00:02:29.343
I wanted to research and
write about for a living.

00:02:29.343 --> 00:02:31.179
As you try to figure
out the impossible task

00:02:31.179 --> 00:02:32.909
of juggling work and family

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:34.453
and you hear over and over and over again

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:35.804
that you just need a lot of help,

00:02:35.804 --> 00:02:36.723
or you just need to be organize,

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.838
or you just need to try
a little bit harder,

00:02:38.838 --> 00:02:41.692
as a very successful woman,
a single mother of three,

00:02:41.692 --> 00:02:43.244
who constantly gets asked the question,

00:02:43.244 --> 00:02:44.945
"How do you do it all?"

00:02:44.945 --> 00:02:46.588
for once I'm going to answer that question

00:02:46.588 --> 00:02:49.507
with 100% honesty here for you now

00:02:49.507 --> 00:02:53.458
because it's just us, 'cause
it's our fireside chat,

00:02:53.458 --> 00:02:56.584
'cause somebody has to tell you the true.

00:02:56.584 --> 00:02:59.504
Shonda, how do you do it all?

00:02:59.504 --> 00:03:01.290
The answer is this.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.123
I don't.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:05.332
Whenever you see me somewhere succeeding

00:03:05.332 --> 00:03:08.989
in one area of my life,
that almost certainly means

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:12.810
I am failing in another area of my life.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.713
If I am killing it on a
Scandal script for work,

00:03:15.713 --> 00:03:18.707
I am probably missing bath
and story time at home.

00:03:18.707 --> 00:03:21.541
If I'm at home sewing my
kids' Halloween costumes,

00:03:21.541 --> 00:03:22.987
I'm probably blowing off a rewrite

00:03:22.987 --> 00:03:24.678
I was supposed to turn in.

00:03:24.678 --> 00:03:26.765
If I'm accepting a prestigious award,

00:03:26.765 --> 00:03:29.509
I'm missing my baby's first swim lesson.

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:32.251
If I'm at my daughter's
debut in her school musical,

00:03:32.251 --> 00:03:34.323
I am missing Sandra Oh's last scene ever

00:03:34.323 --> 00:03:36.368
being filmed at Grey's Anatomy.

00:03:36.368 --> 00:03:37.960
If I am succeeding at one,

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.704
I am inevitably failing at the other.

00:03:40.704 --> 00:03:42.288
That is the trade off.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:44.258
That is the Faustian bargain
one makes with the devil

00:03:44.258 --> 00:03:46.093
that comes with being a
powerful working woman

00:03:46.093 --> 00:03:47.580
who's also a powerful mother.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.565
You never feel 100% okay.

00:03:49.565 --> 00:03:50.987
You never get your sea legs.

00:03:50.987 --> 00:03:52.404
You are always a little nauseous.

00:03:52.404 --> 00:03:54.109
Something is always lost.

00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:56.542
Something is always missing.

00:03:56.542 --> 00:03:59.552
And yet, I want my daughters to see me

00:03:59.552 --> 00:04:01.300
and know me as a woman who works.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.261
I want that example set for them.

00:04:03.261 --> 00:04:04.969
I like how proud they are
when they come to my offices

00:04:04.969 --> 00:04:07.157
and know that they come to ShondaLand.

00:04:07.157 --> 00:04:11.452
There is a land and it is
named after their mother.

00:04:11.452 --> 00:04:13.186
In their world, mothers run companies.

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:15.749
In their world, mothers
own Thursday nights.

00:04:15.749 --> 00:04:17.889
In their world, mothers work.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:20.050
And I am a better mother for it.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.768
The woman I am because
I get to run ShondaLand

00:04:21.768 --> 00:04:22.794
because I get to write all day,

00:04:22.794 --> 00:04:24.389
because I get to spend
my days making things up,

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:27.211
that woman is a better
person and a better mother

00:04:27.211 --> 00:04:28.970
because that woman is happy.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.323
That woman is fulfilled.

00:04:30.323 --> 00:04:31.941
That woman is whole.

00:04:31.941 --> 00:04:33.061
I wouldn't want them to know the me

00:04:33.061 --> 00:04:34.942
who didn't get to do this all day long.

00:04:34.942 --> 00:04:38.512
I wouldn't want them to get to
know the me who wasn't doing.

00:04:38.512 --> 00:04:40.668
So lesson number three is
that anyone who tells you

00:04:40.668 --> 00:04:43.520
they are doing it all perfectly is a liar.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.130
- That inner critic,
you're not good enough,

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.315
you failed, you're not
a good enough mother,

00:04:49.315 --> 00:04:51.307
you're not a good enough person

00:04:51.307 --> 00:04:54.036
is rampant or was rampant in you.

00:04:54.036 --> 00:04:55.360
How did you silence that?

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.511
So, like, you ate the
fried chicken, you suck,

00:04:58.511 --> 00:04:59.344
like, I would do that.

00:04:59.344 --> 00:05:00.250
(audience laughs)

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.209
Yeah, and then you're like,
okay, I ate the fried chicken,

00:05:02.209 --> 00:05:04.719
I might as well eat a pint of ice cream,

00:05:04.719 --> 00:05:07.001
then I might as well, yeah,

00:05:07.001 --> 00:05:08.481
and then the circle begins.

00:05:08.481 --> 00:05:10.129
I can't do anything, I'm a loser,

00:05:10.129 --> 00:05:12.269
and then there it goes.

00:05:12.269 --> 00:05:14.146
- But it's true, I think
we all have that in us,

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:17.951
and I am an extraordinarily
competitive person.

00:05:17.951 --> 00:05:19.687
I come from, so many similarities,

00:05:19.687 --> 00:05:21.237
I come from a big family.

00:05:21.237 --> 00:05:22.893
My big family is very competitive.

00:05:22.893 --> 00:05:25.459
We're literally not allowed
to play Scrabble in my house

00:05:25.459 --> 00:05:26.888
(audience laughs)

00:05:26.888 --> 00:05:28.826
because of fights that break out.

00:05:28.826 --> 00:05:30.391
My mother's like, "You're not allowed."

00:05:30.391 --> 00:05:32.384
And because of that competitiveness,

00:05:32.384 --> 00:05:35.270
I'm really hard on myself,
and I'm the youngest of six,

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.230
so I've sort of been the
bottom of that competitiveness,

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:41.006
and so you're always pushing harder.

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:43.814
That thing makes you intense,

00:05:43.814 --> 00:05:46.380
and it makes you really,
really sort of OCD

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.564
and hard on yourself.

00:05:47.564 --> 00:05:50.708
Part of what I tried to do was

00:05:50.708 --> 00:05:52.988
let it be okay to mess up

00:05:52.988 --> 00:05:55.526
and give myself permission and think

00:05:55.526 --> 00:05:57.141
how would I treat somebody else.

00:05:57.141 --> 00:05:58.540
Why could I treat other people

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.154
better than I could treat myself?

00:06:00.154 --> 00:06:03.637
'Cause I could fight for
anybody who worked for me.

00:06:03.637 --> 00:06:06.120
I could gladiate for anybody at work

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.076
and tell anybody off for anybody else.

00:06:09.076 --> 00:06:11.336
I could treat anybody else wonderfully.

00:06:11.336 --> 00:06:14.644
But when it came to myself, I
let people treat me like crap

00:06:14.644 --> 00:06:16.474
and I treated myself that way,

00:06:16.474 --> 00:06:19.203
and it felt like I
respected myself far less

00:06:19.203 --> 00:06:21.181
than I respected everybody else.

00:06:21.181 --> 00:06:23.945
And so I just tried to respect myself.

00:06:23.945 --> 00:06:24.778
- Easier said than done.

00:06:24.778 --> 00:06:28.868
How do you begin to,
quote, "respect yourself?"

00:06:28.868 --> 00:06:30.829
- A little bit was the self care.

00:06:30.829 --> 00:06:33.004
The more you say yes

00:06:33.004 --> 00:06:35.029
and the more you realize
that you're succeeding

00:06:35.029 --> 00:06:37.044
and that you're overcoming something,

00:06:37.044 --> 00:06:40.392
the better you feel, the
more powerful you feel,

00:06:40.392 --> 00:06:42.257
the more successful you feel,

00:06:42.257 --> 00:06:44.358
and the better you feel
about yourself honestly.

00:06:44.358 --> 00:06:47.001
When I conquered being
afraid of public speaking,

00:06:47.001 --> 00:06:49.168
I started to feel like,
oh, I'm good at that.

00:06:49.168 --> 00:06:51.256
Okay, well, it works.

00:06:51.256 --> 00:06:53.633
You start to feel like you're in control,

00:06:53.633 --> 00:06:55.537
as opposed to feeling like

00:06:55.537 --> 00:06:57.702
it's just set that you're
a loser or something.

00:06:57.702 --> 00:06:59.853
I'm a writer, I'm supposed
to be behind the scenes,

00:06:59.853 --> 00:07:01.694
and everybody else is supposed
to be in front of the stuff.

00:07:01.694 --> 00:07:04.176
- Now she's finally in
front of the cameras.

00:07:04.176 --> 00:07:07.276
The famously private woman
talking about her epic struggles

00:07:07.276 --> 00:07:10.274
with anxiety and her
fear of public speaking.

00:07:10.274 --> 00:07:11.774
What physiologically happens

00:07:11.774 --> 00:07:14.468
when you're having that
stage four panic attack?

00:07:14.468 --> 00:07:16.672
- There's a little bit
of panicked breathing.

00:07:16.672 --> 00:07:18.253
There's a lot of pacing back and forth

00:07:18.253 --> 00:07:19.829
and mumbling to myself.

00:07:19.829 --> 00:07:20.662
- [Interviewer] Despite being invited

00:07:20.662 --> 00:07:23.233
to every glamorous party in Hollywood,

00:07:23.233 --> 00:07:26.164
she'd make excuses, saying
she was just too busy

00:07:26.164 --> 00:07:28.579
until one day her sister provoked her.

00:07:28.579 --> 00:07:30.570
- I was sort of going on and on and on

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.028
about like all of the
invitations that I'd received

00:07:33.028 --> 00:07:35.444
and my sister finally
sort of cut in and said,

00:07:35.444 --> 00:07:38.163
"Are you going to say yes
to any of these things?"

00:07:38.163 --> 00:07:40.004
And I remember being very taken aback

00:07:40.004 --> 00:07:43.581
and saying, "No, I'm busy, I can't."

00:07:43.581 --> 00:07:46.356
And her sort of looking
at me like that's insane

00:07:46.356 --> 00:07:47.542
and at some point just saying,

00:07:47.542 --> 00:07:50.055
"You never say yes to anything."

00:07:50.055 --> 00:07:52.383
And those words really sticking with me

00:07:52.383 --> 00:07:53.743
because she was right.

00:07:53.743 --> 00:07:56.239
- And so this became a campaign to say yes

00:07:56.239 --> 00:07:57.980
to the things that scared you.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.006
- Yeah, to say yes to
things that freaked me out.

00:08:00.006 --> 00:08:03.086
- And when you started saying
yes, what started to happen?

00:08:03.086 --> 00:08:04.662
- It's the doing of the thing

00:08:04.662 --> 00:08:06.731
that sort of undoes the fear.

00:08:06.731 --> 00:08:09.645
The idea now to me that I
wanted to be Toni Morrison

00:08:09.645 --> 00:08:11.395
seems very sad really

00:08:13.473 --> 00:08:16.279
'cause, right, Toni Morrison's
not giving up her job.

00:08:16.279 --> 00:08:18.357
Toni Morrison's busy being Toni Morrison.

00:08:18.357 --> 00:08:20.958
That job opening is not available.

00:08:20.958 --> 00:08:23.112
And it sort of led me to being

00:08:23.112 --> 00:08:25.321
more authentically me actually

00:08:25.321 --> 00:08:27.469
by really just sort of finding a path

00:08:27.469 --> 00:08:31.013
that was like here's an opening,
I'm going to go for that,

00:08:31.013 --> 00:08:32.631
and that's how I discovered
writing in television

00:08:32.631 --> 00:08:35.500
and that's how I discovered
what kind of writing fit me.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:36.946
- Yeah, 'cause you
didn't expect to do this

00:08:36.946 --> 00:08:37.925
when you were at Dartmouth.

00:08:37.925 --> 00:08:40.370
- No, and let me tell you,
I tried to write a novel,

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.823
like a really intricate
Toni Morrison-esque novel,

00:08:42.823 --> 00:08:44.622
and it is terrible.

00:08:44.622 --> 00:08:46.327
I am not Toni Morrison.

00:08:46.327 --> 00:08:48.639
But this feels right to me,

00:08:48.639 --> 00:08:52.530
and when I started writing
television, it felt like, oh,

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.371
this is what I should've
been doing all my life.

00:08:54.371 --> 00:08:56.732
It was like a light bulb went off.

00:08:56.732 --> 00:08:58.578
- What makes a good leader in your mind?

00:08:58.578 --> 00:09:01.245
What have you learned about leadership

00:09:01.245 --> 00:09:03.590
and what is a takeaway for people here

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:07.227
about a first step towards
being a better leader?

00:09:07.227 --> 00:09:09.993
- I don't think that you have to be me.

00:09:09.993 --> 00:09:12.466
I really only feel like
I started truly learning

00:09:12.466 --> 00:09:16.528
how to be a leader about
two or three years ago

00:09:16.528 --> 00:09:17.834
partially when this process started

00:09:17.834 --> 00:09:20.084
when I got braver about it.

00:09:21.214 --> 00:09:25.381
I think it's a willing to
really delve into this stuff,

00:09:26.361 --> 00:09:28.232
to have the difficult conversations,

00:09:28.232 --> 00:09:29.863
that's another part of the book,

00:09:29.863 --> 00:09:32.716
of really being willing to
have those conversations

00:09:32.716 --> 00:09:33.886
that nobody wants to have

00:09:33.886 --> 00:09:35.825
that we spend all of our time avoiding,

00:09:35.825 --> 00:09:37.143
sitting down with the people you work with

00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:40.662
and saying, "Here's the
deal" or "What's going on?"

00:09:40.662 --> 00:09:42.710
Or all of those things that we spend

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.316
all of our time avoiding,
just jumping right in,

00:09:46.316 --> 00:09:47.910
and those things solve problems.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.764
Those things make life so much easier.

00:09:49.764 --> 00:09:51.565
Those things cross bridges in ways

00:09:51.565 --> 00:09:53.693
that you never thought would happen.

00:09:53.693 --> 00:09:55.772
That for me has been the
single greatest thing

00:09:55.772 --> 00:09:57.725
that has changed the way
I work with everybody

00:09:57.725 --> 00:09:59.628
because now everyone feels like,

00:09:59.628 --> 00:10:01.916
A, I'm going to tell them the truth,

00:10:01.916 --> 00:10:04.493
and, B, they can tell me anything.

00:10:04.493 --> 00:10:07.092
I love the idea that I'm not somebody

00:10:07.092 --> 00:10:09.114
that they're afraid of.

00:10:09.114 --> 00:10:10.911
There's some weird world in which

00:10:10.911 --> 00:10:12.048
anybody gives you a compliment

00:10:12.048 --> 00:10:14.762
your response is, oh, it's
not that big of a deal.

00:10:14.762 --> 00:10:16.518
- And even you, Shonda Rhimes,

00:10:16.518 --> 00:10:18.819
creator of all this fabulousness,

00:10:18.819 --> 00:10:20.491
was like, oh, no, I was lucky.

00:10:20.491 --> 00:10:21.707
- It makes you feel arrogant

00:10:21.707 --> 00:10:24.396
to sort of somehow acknowledge
that you had some part in it,

00:10:24.396 --> 00:10:26.007
which is ridiculous.

00:10:26.007 --> 00:10:28.063
Own who you are and what you've done.

00:10:28.063 --> 00:10:29.108
Be proud of it.

00:10:29.108 --> 00:10:32.083
- You talk in the book
that you used to shy away

00:10:32.083 --> 00:10:36.188
from difficult conversations
and that you realized

00:10:36.188 --> 00:10:38.961
that the more you wanted to say something

00:10:38.961 --> 00:10:41.097
but you didn't so you ate it,

00:10:41.097 --> 00:10:43.996
literally, you ate it to numb it, right,

00:10:43.996 --> 00:10:46.867
and that the first time you actually said

00:10:46.867 --> 00:10:51.021
to somebody like, "That
doesn't work for me"

00:10:51.021 --> 00:10:54.616
or you gave yourself permission to say no

00:10:54.616 --> 00:10:59.248
in the year of yes, that all
of a sudden you felt free.

00:10:59.248 --> 00:11:02.674
People are afraid to say,
"That doesn't work for me"

00:11:02.674 --> 00:11:05.035
or "No" because they'll lose a friend,

00:11:05.035 --> 00:11:08.676
they may lose a spouse,
they may lose a child.

00:11:08.676 --> 00:11:10.301
Tough love, as we call it,

00:11:10.301 --> 00:11:13.666
or saying, "That doesn't work for me"

00:11:13.666 --> 00:11:15.869
is a really hard thing to say.

00:11:15.869 --> 00:11:18.530
- But the thing that we fear will happen,

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.063
you're still fearing it
while you're avoiding it.

00:11:21.063 --> 00:11:23.728
So the entire time you're
not having the conversation

00:11:23.728 --> 00:11:25.504
or saying the thing you need to say,

00:11:25.504 --> 00:11:28.695
you're basically terrified that
that thing's going to happen.

00:11:28.695 --> 00:11:31.092
So it's in there and on
you and pressing on you,

00:11:31.092 --> 00:11:34.497
and for me, I was just
eating a whole lot of stuff.

00:11:34.497 --> 00:11:36.045
But once you have the conversation,

00:11:36.045 --> 00:11:38.731
I had one really hard conversation

00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:41.200
that ended with a really
close friend of mine

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.262
just saying some of the worst things to me

00:11:43.262 --> 00:11:45.360
I never heard anybody say,

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:47.577
and instead of feeling awful,

00:11:47.577 --> 00:11:50.652
I found myself feeling
invigorated and relieved

00:11:50.652 --> 00:11:52.458
and a little bit exhilarated

00:11:52.458 --> 00:11:54.776
because I had told her how I felt

00:11:54.776 --> 00:11:57.516
and she had told me who she was

00:11:57.516 --> 00:12:00.082
and now that I knew that, I was like,

00:12:00.082 --> 00:12:01.626
this is like a superpower

00:12:01.626 --> 00:12:02.459
(audience laughs)

00:12:02.459 --> 00:12:05.994
because you unmask people immediately

00:12:05.994 --> 00:12:09.827
and that was such a
relief and felt so simple.

00:12:10.948 --> 00:12:14.527
Across the field of a difficult
conversation lies peace.

00:12:14.527 --> 00:12:18.242
So a while ago, I tried an experiment.

00:12:18.242 --> 00:12:19.986
For one year, I would say yes

00:12:19.986 --> 00:12:22.384
to all the things that scared me.

00:12:22.384 --> 00:12:23.816
Anything that made me nervous,

00:12:23.816 --> 00:12:25.710
took me out of my comfort zone,

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:28.082
I forced myself to say yes to.

00:12:28.082 --> 00:12:29.730
Did I want to speak in public?

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:30.730
No, but yes.

00:12:31.964 --> 00:12:33.721
Did I want to be on live TV?

00:12:33.721 --> 00:12:34.721
No, but yes.

00:12:36.208 --> 00:12:38.009
Did I want to try acting?

00:12:38.009 --> 00:12:41.080
No, no, no, but yes, yes, yes.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.089
And a crazy thing happened.

00:12:43.089 --> 00:12:45.824
The very act of doing
the thing that scared me

00:12:45.824 --> 00:12:49.167
undid the fear, made it not scary.

00:12:49.167 --> 00:12:53.334
My fear of public speaking,
my social anxiety, poof, gone.

00:12:54.322 --> 00:12:57.252
It's amazing, the power of one word.

00:12:57.252 --> 00:12:59.448
Yes changed my life.

00:12:59.448 --> 00:13:01.067
Yes changed me.

00:13:01.067 --> 00:13:03.501
But there was one particular yes

00:13:03.501 --> 00:13:06.513
that affected my life in
the most profound way,

00:13:06.513 --> 00:13:08.353
in a way I never imagined,

00:13:08.353 --> 00:13:12.150
and it started with a
question from my toddler.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:13.645
I have these three amazing daughters,

00:13:13.645 --> 00:13:16.277
Harper, Beckett and Emerson,
and Emerson's a toddler

00:13:16.277 --> 00:13:18.782
who inexplicably refers
to everyone as honey

00:13:18.782 --> 00:13:20.584
as though she's a Southern waitress.

00:13:20.584 --> 00:13:21.828
(audience laughs)

00:13:21.828 --> 00:13:24.669
"Honey, I'm going to need
some milk for my sippy cup."

00:13:24.669 --> 00:13:26.563
(audience laughs)

00:13:26.563 --> 00:13:29.149
The Southern waitress
asked me to play with her

00:13:29.149 --> 00:13:31.232
one evening when I was
on my way somewhere,

00:13:31.232 --> 00:13:32.619
and I said, "Yes."

00:13:32.619 --> 00:13:33.998
And that yes was the beginning

00:13:33.998 --> 00:13:36.613
of a new way of life for my family.

00:13:36.613 --> 00:13:38.572
I made a vow that from now on,

00:13:38.572 --> 00:13:41.082
every time one of my
children asks me to play,

00:13:41.082 --> 00:13:43.491
no matter what I'm doing
or where I'm going,

00:13:43.491 --> 00:13:45.908
I say yes, every single time.

00:13:48.001 --> 00:13:48.834
Almost.

00:13:48.834 --> 00:13:51.557
I'm not perfect at it, but
I try hard to practice it.

00:13:51.557 --> 00:13:54.036
And it's had a magical effect on me,

00:13:54.036 --> 00:13:57.234
on my children, on our family.

00:13:57.234 --> 00:14:00.154
But it's also had a stunning side effect,

00:14:00.154 --> 00:14:03.765
and it wasn't until recently
that I fully understood it,

00:14:03.765 --> 00:14:05.597
that I understood that saying yes

00:14:05.597 --> 00:14:10.469
to playing with my children
likely saved my career.

00:14:10.469 --> 00:14:13.639
See, I have what most people
would call a dream job.

00:14:13.639 --> 00:14:16.408
I'm a writer, I imagine, I
make stuff up for a living.

00:14:16.408 --> 00:14:17.836
Dream job.

00:14:17.836 --> 00:14:19.189
No.

00:14:19.189 --> 00:14:20.988
I'm a titan.

00:14:20.988 --> 00:14:22.309
Dream job.

00:14:22.309 --> 00:14:23.500
I create television.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:24.728
I executive produce television.

00:14:24.728 --> 00:14:27.976
I make television, a
great deal of television.

00:14:27.976 --> 00:14:29.600
In one way or another, this TV season,

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.626
I'm responsible for
bringing about 70 hours

00:14:31.626 --> 00:14:33.288
of programming to the world.

00:14:33.288 --> 00:14:35.279
Four television programs, 70 hours of TV.

00:14:35.279 --> 00:14:36.112
(audience applauds)

00:14:36.112 --> 00:14:38.921
Three shows in production
at a time, sometimes four.

00:14:38.921 --> 00:14:42.415
Each show creates hundreds of
jobs that didn't exist before.

00:14:42.415 --> 00:14:44.393
The budget for one episode
of network television

00:14:44.393 --> 00:14:46.657
can be anywhere from three
to six million dollars.

00:14:46.657 --> 00:14:48.298
Let's just say five.

00:14:48.298 --> 00:14:50.592
A new episode made every
nine days times four shows,

00:14:50.592 --> 00:14:53.388
so every nine days that's $20
million worth of television,

00:14:53.388 --> 00:14:55.387
four television programs, 70 hours of TV,

00:14:55.387 --> 00:14:57.407
three shows in production
at a time, sometimes four,

00:14:57.407 --> 00:15:00.060
16 episodes going on at all times.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.051
24 episodes of Grey's,
21 episodes of Scandal,

00:15:02.051 --> 00:15:03.410
15 episodes of How To
Get Away With Murder,

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.546
10 episodes of The Catch,
that's 70 hours of TV,

00:15:05.546 --> 00:15:08.218
that's $350 million for a season.

00:15:08.218 --> 00:15:10.068
In America, my television shows

00:15:10.068 --> 00:15:12.299
are back to back to
back on Thursday night.

00:15:12.299 --> 00:15:15.151
Around the world, my shows
air in 256 territories

00:15:15.151 --> 00:15:17.889
in 67 languages for an
audience of 30 million people.

00:15:17.889 --> 00:15:22.488
My brain is global, and 45
hours of that 70 hours of TV

00:15:22.488 --> 00:15:24.740
are shows I personally
created and not just produced,

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:27.418
so on top of everything
else, I need to find time,

00:15:27.418 --> 00:15:30.768
real quiet, creative
time, to gather my fans

00:15:30.768 --> 00:15:33.848
around the campfire and tell my stories.

00:15:33.848 --> 00:15:35.800
Four television programs, 70 hours of TV,

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:38.287
three shows in production
at a time, sometimes four,

00:15:38.287 --> 00:15:42.378
$350 million, campfires
burning all over the world.

00:15:42.378 --> 00:15:44.462
You know who else is doing that?

00:15:44.462 --> 00:15:47.288
Nobody, so like I said, I'm a titan.

00:15:47.288 --> 00:15:48.225
Dream job.

00:15:48.225 --> 00:15:49.289
(audience members cheer)

00:15:49.289 --> 00:15:52.018
Now, I don't tell you this to impress you.

00:15:52.018 --> 00:15:54.117
I tell you this because
I know what you think of

00:15:54.117 --> 00:15:56.133
when you hear the word writer.

00:15:56.133 --> 00:15:57.845
I tell you this so that
all of you out there

00:15:57.845 --> 00:16:00.619
who work so hard,
whether you run a company

00:16:00.619 --> 00:16:05.102
or a country or a classroom
or a store or a home,

00:16:05.102 --> 00:16:07.757
take me seriously when
I talk about working,

00:16:07.757 --> 00:16:09.822
so you'll get that I
don't peck at a computer

00:16:09.822 --> 00:16:13.552
and imagine all day, so
you'll hear me when I say

00:16:13.552 --> 00:16:17.748
that I understand that a dream
job is not about dreaming.

00:16:17.748 --> 00:16:20.627
It's all job, all work, all reality,

00:16:20.627 --> 00:16:23.038
all blood, all sweat, no tears.

00:16:23.038 --> 00:16:26.288
I work a lot, very hard, and I love it.

00:16:27.589 --> 00:16:29.809
When I'm hard at work,
when I'm deep in it,

00:16:29.809 --> 00:16:32.294
there is no other feeling.

00:16:32.294 --> 00:16:34.047
For me, my work is at all times

00:16:34.047 --> 00:16:35.840
building a nation out of thin air.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:37.369
It is manning the troops.

00:16:37.369 --> 00:16:38.616
It is painting a canvas.

00:16:38.616 --> 00:16:39.907
It is hitting every high note.

00:16:39.907 --> 00:16:40.975
It is running a marathon.

00:16:40.975 --> 00:16:42.920
It is being Beyonce.

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.658
And it is all of those
things at the same time.

00:16:46.658 --> 00:16:48.226
I love working.

00:16:48.226 --> 00:16:50.609
It is creative and
mechanical and exhausting

00:16:50.609 --> 00:16:52.585
and exhilarating and
hilarious and disturbing

00:16:52.585 --> 00:16:55.289
and clinical and maternal
and cruel and judicious,

00:16:55.289 --> 00:16:59.348
and what makes it all so good is the hum.

00:16:59.348 --> 00:17:00.793
There is some kind of shift inside me

00:17:00.793 --> 00:17:02.576
when the work gets good.

00:17:02.576 --> 00:17:06.239
A hum begins in my brain,
and it grows and it grows

00:17:06.239 --> 00:17:08.154
and that hum sounds like the open road

00:17:08.154 --> 00:17:11.123
and I could drive it forever.

00:17:11.123 --> 00:17:14.251
And a lot of people, when
I try to explain the hum,

00:17:14.251 --> 00:17:16.249
they assume that I'm
talking about the writing,

00:17:16.249 --> 00:17:17.606
that my writing brings me joy.

00:17:17.606 --> 00:17:21.102
And don't get me wrong, it does.

00:17:21.102 --> 00:17:24.829
But the hum, it wasn't until
I started making television

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:27.403
that I started working, working and making

00:17:27.403 --> 00:17:30.125
and building and creating
and collaborating,

00:17:30.125 --> 00:17:32.331
that I discovered this thing, this buzz,

00:17:32.331 --> 00:17:33.998
this rush, this hum.

00:17:35.534 --> 00:17:37.542
The hum is more than writing.

00:17:37.542 --> 00:17:39.285
The hum is action and activity.

00:17:39.285 --> 00:17:40.737
The hum is a drug.

00:17:40.737 --> 00:17:41.742
The hum is music.

00:17:41.742 --> 00:17:43.391
The hum is light and air.

00:17:43.391 --> 00:17:47.374
The hum is God's whisper right in my ear.

00:17:47.374 --> 00:17:49.806
And when you have a hum like that,

00:17:49.806 --> 00:17:52.318
you can't help but strive for greatness.

00:17:52.318 --> 00:17:55.022
That feeling, you can't help but strive

00:17:55.022 --> 00:17:57.826
for greatness at any cost.

00:17:57.826 --> 00:17:59.849
That's called the hum.

00:17:59.849 --> 00:18:03.315
Or, maybe it's called being a workaholic.

00:18:03.315 --> 00:18:05.075
(audience laughs)

00:18:05.075 --> 00:18:07.890
Maybe it's called genius.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.229
Maybe it's called ego.

00:18:10.229 --> 00:18:13.331
Maybe it's just fear of failure.

00:18:13.331 --> 00:18:14.979
I don't know.

00:18:14.979 --> 00:18:18.846
I just know that I'm
not built for failure,

00:18:18.846 --> 00:18:21.503
and I just know that I love the hum.

00:18:21.503 --> 00:18:23.530
I just know that I want
to tell you I'm a titan,

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:26.261
and I know that I don't
want to question it.

00:18:26.261 --> 00:18:28.781
But here's the thing.

00:18:28.781 --> 00:18:31.555
The more successful I
become, the more shows,

00:18:31.555 --> 00:18:34.213
the more episodes, the
more barriers broken,

00:18:34.213 --> 00:18:37.610
the more work there is to do,
the more balls in the air,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:40.507
the more eyes on me,
the more history stares,

00:18:40.507 --> 00:18:42.979
the more expectations there are.

00:18:42.979 --> 00:18:47.647
The more I work to be successful,
the more I need to work.

00:18:47.647 --> 00:18:49.313
And what did I say about work?

00:18:49.313 --> 00:18:51.350
I love working, right?

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:52.941
The nation I'm building,
the marathon I'm running,

00:18:52.941 --> 00:18:54.526
the troops, the canvas, the high note,

00:18:54.526 --> 00:18:56.693
the hum, the hum, the hum.

00:18:57.869 --> 00:18:58.723
I like that hum.

00:18:58.723 --> 00:18:59.861
I love that hum.

00:18:59.861 --> 00:19:00.985
I need that hum.

00:19:00.985 --> 00:19:02.152
I am that hum.

00:19:03.257 --> 00:19:05.424
Am I nothing but that hum?

00:19:07.077 --> 00:19:09.404
And then the hum stopped.

00:19:09.404 --> 00:19:13.122
Overworked, overused,
overdone, burned out.

00:19:13.122 --> 00:19:14.455
The hum stopped.

00:19:16.812 --> 00:19:19.179
Now, my three daughters
are used to the truth

00:19:19.179 --> 00:19:22.284
that their mother is a
single working titan.

00:19:22.284 --> 00:19:24.500
Harper tells people,
"My mom won't be there,

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:26.904
"but you can text my nanny."

00:19:26.904 --> 00:19:31.444
And Emerson says, "Honey, I'm
wanting to go to ShondaLand."

00:19:31.444 --> 00:19:32.774
They're children of a titan.

00:19:32.774 --> 00:19:35.234
They're baby titans.

00:19:35.234 --> 00:19:39.459
They were 12, three, and
one when the hum stopped.

00:19:39.459 --> 00:19:41.537
The hum of the engine died.

00:19:41.537 --> 00:19:42.664
I stopped loving work.

00:19:42.664 --> 00:19:43.906
I couldn't restart the engine.

00:19:43.906 --> 00:19:45.968
The hum would not come back.

00:19:45.968 --> 00:19:47.468
My hum was broken.

00:19:49.183 --> 00:19:51.206
I was doing the same things I always did,

00:19:51.206 --> 00:19:53.985
all the same titan work, 15-hour days,

00:19:53.985 --> 00:19:55.210
working straight through the weekends,

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:56.680
no regrets, never surrender,

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.416
a titan never sleeps, a titan never quits,

00:19:58.416 --> 00:20:01.713
full hearts, clear eyes, yada, whatever.

00:20:01.713 --> 00:20:03.463
But there was no hum.

00:20:04.563 --> 00:20:06.396
Inside me was silence.

00:20:07.773 --> 00:20:09.770
Four television programs, 70 hours of TV,

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:11.966
three shows in production
at a time, sometimes four.

00:20:11.966 --> 00:20:14.133
Four television programs, 70 hours of TV,

00:20:14.133 --> 00:20:16.396
three shows in production
at a time, sometimes four.

00:20:16.396 --> 00:20:18.690
I was the perfect titan.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:22.240
I was a titan you could
take home to your mother.

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:23.459
All the colors were the same,

00:20:23.459 --> 00:20:26.300
and I was no longer having any fun.

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.459
And it was my life.

00:20:28.459 --> 00:20:29.812
It was all I did.

00:20:29.812 --> 00:20:33.347
I was the hum, and the hum was me.

00:20:33.347 --> 00:20:35.971
So what do you do when the thing you do,

00:20:35.971 --> 00:20:39.614
the work you love, starts
to taste like dust?

00:20:39.614 --> 00:20:41.540
Now I know somebody's out there thinking,

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:43.795
"Cry me a river, stupid
writer titan lady."

00:20:43.795 --> 00:20:45.835
(audience laughs)

00:20:45.835 --> 00:20:49.406
But you know, you do, if
you make, if you work,

00:20:49.406 --> 00:20:52.207
if you love what you do, being
a teacher, being a banker,

00:20:52.207 --> 00:20:54.935
being a mother, being a
painter, being Bill Gates,

00:20:54.935 --> 00:20:56.446
if you simply love another person

00:20:56.446 --> 00:20:59.971
and that gives you the
hum, if you know the hum,

00:20:59.971 --> 00:21:01.747
if you know what the hum feels like,

00:21:01.747 --> 00:21:03.994
if you have been to the hum,

00:21:03.994 --> 00:21:06.661
when the hum stops, who are you?

00:21:08.404 --> 00:21:09.487
What are you?

00:21:10.466 --> 00:21:11.859
What am I?

00:21:11.859 --> 00:21:13.442
Am I still a titan?

00:21:14.755 --> 00:21:17.078
If the song of my heart ceases to play,

00:21:17.078 --> 00:21:19.495
can I survive in the silence?

00:21:21.522 --> 00:21:25.626
And then my Southern waitress
toddler asks me a question.

00:21:25.626 --> 00:21:28.360
I'm on my way out the door, I'm late,

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:31.193
and she says, "Momma, want to play?"

00:21:32.557 --> 00:21:35.935
And I'm just about to say no
when I realize two things.

00:21:35.935 --> 00:21:39.120
One, I'm supposed to
say yes to everything,

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:43.290
and two, my Southern waitress
didn't call me honey.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:46.370
She's not calling everyone honey anymore.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.049
When did that happen?

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:50.465
I'm missing it, being a
titan and mourning my hum,

00:21:50.465 --> 00:21:54.180
and here she is changing
right before my eyes.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:57.707
And so she says, "Momma, want to play?"

00:21:57.707 --> 00:21:59.843
And I say, "Yes."

00:21:59.843 --> 00:22:02.261
There's nothing special about it.

00:22:02.261 --> 00:22:04.882
We play, and we're joined by her sisters,

00:22:04.882 --> 00:22:05.972
and there's a lot of laughing,

00:22:05.972 --> 00:22:09.501
and I give a dramatic reading
from the book Everybody Poops.

00:22:09.501 --> 00:22:10.334
(audience laughs)

00:22:10.334 --> 00:22:12.599
Nothing out of the ordinary.

00:22:12.599 --> 00:22:14.805
And yet, it is extraordinary

00:22:14.805 --> 00:22:17.261
because in my pain and my panic,

00:22:17.261 --> 00:22:19.411
in the homelessness of my humlessness,

00:22:19.411 --> 00:22:22.502
I have nothing to do but pay attention.

00:22:22.502 --> 00:22:23.864
I focus.

00:22:23.864 --> 00:22:24.781
I am still.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.730
The nation I'm building,
the marathon I'm running,

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:32.144
the troops, the canvas, the
high note does not exist.

00:22:32.144 --> 00:22:34.960
All that exists are sticky
fingers and gooey kisses

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:38.900
and tiny voices and crayons
and that song about letting go

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:41.659
of whatever it is that Frozen
girl needs to let go of.

00:22:41.659 --> 00:22:43.995
(audience laughs)

00:22:43.995 --> 00:22:46.495
It's all peace and simplicity.

00:22:47.613 --> 00:22:49.948
The air is so rare in this place for me

00:22:49.948 --> 00:22:51.778
that I can barely breathe.

00:22:51.778 --> 00:22:54.539
I can barely believe I'm breathing.

00:22:54.539 --> 00:22:56.956
Play is the opposite of work.

00:22:58.122 --> 00:22:59.372
And I am happy.

00:23:00.398 --> 00:23:01.609
Something in me loosens.

00:23:01.609 --> 00:23:06.450
A door in my brain swings open,
and a rush of energy comes.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:10.856
And it's not instantaneous,
but it happens, it does happen.

00:23:10.856 --> 00:23:12.308
I feel it.

00:23:12.308 --> 00:23:14.182
A hum creeps back.

00:23:14.182 --> 00:23:17.441
Not at full volume,
barely there, it's quiet,

00:23:17.441 --> 00:23:20.094
and I have to stay very still
to hear it, but it is there.

00:23:20.094 --> 00:23:22.011
Not the hum, but a hum.

00:23:23.638 --> 00:23:27.533
And now I feel like I know
a very magical secret.

00:23:27.533 --> 00:23:29.974
Well, let's not get carried away.

00:23:29.974 --> 00:23:31.837
It's just love.

00:23:31.837 --> 00:23:33.921
That's all it is.

00:23:33.921 --> 00:23:35.071
No magic.

00:23:35.071 --> 00:23:35.904
No secret.

00:23:35.904 --> 00:23:37.778
It's just love.

00:23:37.778 --> 00:23:40.274
It's just something we forgot.

00:23:40.274 --> 00:23:43.774
The hum, the work hum,
the hum of the titan,

00:23:43.774 --> 00:23:45.678
that's just a replacement.

00:23:45.678 --> 00:23:48.024
If I have to ask you who I am,

00:23:48.024 --> 00:23:50.038
if I have to tell you who I am,

00:23:50.038 --> 00:23:52.376
if I describe myself in terms of shows

00:23:52.376 --> 00:23:56.814
and hours of television and how
globally badass my brain is,

00:23:56.814 --> 00:24:00.417
I have forgotten what the real hum is.

00:24:00.417 --> 00:24:04.592
The hum is not power and the
hum is not work-specific.

00:24:04.592 --> 00:24:06.699
The hum is joy-specific.

00:24:06.699 --> 00:24:08.952
The real hum is love-specific.

00:24:08.952 --> 00:24:10.641
The hum is the electricity that comes

00:24:10.641 --> 00:24:12.555
from being excited by life.

00:24:12.555 --> 00:24:15.407
The real hum is confidence and peace.

00:24:15.407 --> 00:24:17.944
The real hum ignores the stare of history

00:24:17.944 --> 00:24:18.946
and the balls in the air

00:24:18.946 --> 00:24:20.938
and the expectation and the pressure.

00:24:20.938 --> 00:24:24.282
The real hum is singular and original.

00:24:24.282 --> 00:24:26.225
The real hum is God's whisper in my ear,

00:24:26.225 --> 00:24:28.587
but maybe God was
whispering the wrong words,

00:24:28.587 --> 00:24:29.575
because which one of the gods

00:24:29.575 --> 00:24:32.495
was telling me I was a titan?

00:24:32.495 --> 00:24:33.745
It's just love.

00:24:34.584 --> 00:24:38.751
We could all use a little
more love, a lot more love.

00:24:40.024 --> 00:24:44.875
Any time my child asks me
to play, I will say yes.

00:24:44.875 --> 00:24:46.832
I make it a firm rule for one reason,

00:24:46.832 --> 00:24:47.972
to give myself permission,

00:24:47.972 --> 00:24:50.933
to free me from all of
my workaholic guilt.

00:24:50.933 --> 00:24:53.144
It's a law, so I don't have a choice,

00:24:53.144 --> 00:24:57.759
and I don't have a choice,
not if I want to feel the hum.

00:24:57.759 --> 00:25:01.926
I wish it were that easy,
but I'm not good at playing.

00:25:03.028 --> 00:25:04.263
I don't like it.

00:25:04.263 --> 00:25:06.202
(audience chuckles)

00:25:06.202 --> 00:25:08.472
I'm not interested in doing it

00:25:08.472 --> 00:25:11.122
the way I'm interested in doing work.

00:25:11.122 --> 00:25:14.448
The truth is incredibly humbling
and humiliating to face.

00:25:14.448 --> 00:25:16.091
I don't like playing.

00:25:16.091 --> 00:25:18.932
I work all the time
because I like working.

00:25:18.932 --> 00:25:22.765
I like working more than
I like being at home.

00:25:23.695 --> 00:25:28.461
Facing that fact is incredibly
difficult to handle,

00:25:28.461 --> 00:25:31.136
because what kind of person

00:25:31.136 --> 00:25:34.303
likes working more than being at home?

00:25:35.393 --> 00:25:36.226
Well, me.

00:25:37.589 --> 00:25:40.863
I mean, let's be honest,
I call myself a titan.

00:25:40.863 --> 00:25:42.205
I've got issues.

00:25:42.205 --> 00:25:44.034
(audience laughs)

00:25:44.034 --> 00:25:46.887
And one of those issues
isn't that I am too relaxed.

00:25:46.887 --> 00:25:49.078
(audience laughs)

00:25:49.078 --> 00:25:53.461
We run around the yard, up
and back and up and back.

00:25:53.461 --> 00:25:55.828
We have 30-second dance parties.

00:25:55.828 --> 00:25:56.921
We sing show tunes.

00:25:56.921 --> 00:25:58.956
We play with balls.

00:25:58.956 --> 00:26:01.052
I blow bubbles and they pop them.

00:26:01.052 --> 00:26:03.281
And I feel stiff and delirious

00:26:03.281 --> 00:26:06.525
and confused most of the time.

00:26:06.525 --> 00:26:09.839
I itch for my cell phone always.

00:26:09.839 --> 00:26:11.558
But it is okay.

00:26:11.558 --> 00:26:13.317
My tiny humans show me how to live

00:26:13.317 --> 00:26:16.511
and the hum of the universe fills me up.

00:26:16.511 --> 00:26:19.415
I play and I play until I begin to wonder

00:26:19.415 --> 00:26:23.211
why we ever stop playing
in the first place.

00:26:23.211 --> 00:26:25.179
You can do it too.

00:26:25.179 --> 00:26:29.341
Say yes every time your
child asks you to play.

00:26:29.341 --> 00:26:32.156
Are you thinking that maybe
I'm an idiot in diamond shoes?

00:26:32.156 --> 00:26:35.007
You're right, but you can still do this.

00:26:35.007 --> 00:26:36.736
You have time.

00:26:36.736 --> 00:26:38.012
You know why?

00:26:38.012 --> 00:26:40.371
'Cause you're not Rihanna
and you're not a Muppet.

00:26:40.371 --> 00:26:43.012
Your child does not think
you're that interesting.

00:26:43.012 --> 00:26:44.075
(audience laughs)

00:26:44.075 --> 00:26:45.987
You only need 15 minutes.

00:26:45.987 --> 00:26:48.750
My two- and four-year-old
only ever want to play with me

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:51.327
for about 15 minutes or so
before they think to themselves

00:26:51.327 --> 00:26:53.285
they want to do something else.

00:26:53.285 --> 00:26:56.861
It's an amazing 15 minutes,
but it's 15 minutes.

00:26:56.861 --> 00:26:58.611
If I'm not a ladybug or a piece of candy,

00:26:58.611 --> 00:27:00.611
I'm invisible after 15 minutes.

00:27:00.611 --> 00:27:01.779
(audience laughs)

00:27:01.779 --> 00:27:02.875
And my 13-year-old,

00:27:02.875 --> 00:27:06.778
if I can get a 13-year-old
to talk to me for 15 minutes,

00:27:06.778 --> 00:27:08.137
I'm Parent of the Year.

00:27:08.137 --> 00:27:09.745
(audience laughs)

00:27:09.745 --> 00:27:11.457
15 minutes is all you need.

00:27:11.457 --> 00:27:13.463
I can totally pull off 15 minutes

00:27:13.463 --> 00:27:16.843
of uninterrupted time on my worst day.

00:27:16.843 --> 00:27:18.915
Uninterrupted is the key.

00:27:18.915 --> 00:27:21.892
No cell phone, no laundry, no anything.

00:27:21.892 --> 00:27:22.725
You have a busy life.

00:27:22.725 --> 00:27:23.558
You have to get dinner on the table.

00:27:23.558 --> 00:27:24.737
You have to force them to bathe.

00:27:24.737 --> 00:27:26.904
But you can do 15 minutes.

00:27:27.754 --> 00:27:30.136
My kids are my happy
place, they're my world,

00:27:30.136 --> 00:27:32.713
but it doesn't have to be your kids,

00:27:32.713 --> 00:27:33.988
the fuel that feeds your hum,

00:27:33.988 --> 00:27:36.844
the place where life feels
more good than not good.

00:27:36.844 --> 00:27:40.592
It's not about playing with
your kids, it's about joy.

00:27:40.592 --> 00:27:43.868
It's about playing in general.

00:27:43.868 --> 00:27:45.702
Give yourself the 15 minutes.

00:27:45.702 --> 00:27:48.000
Find what makes you feel good.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:51.500
Just figure it out and play in that arena.

00:27:52.644 --> 00:27:53.759
I'm not perfect at it.

00:27:53.759 --> 00:27:56.836
In fact, I fail as often as I succeed,

00:27:56.836 --> 00:28:00.367
seeing friends, reading
books, staring into space.

00:28:00.367 --> 00:28:02.247
"Want to play?" starts to become shorthand

00:28:02.247 --> 00:28:05.116
for indulging myself
in ways I'd given up on

00:28:05.116 --> 00:28:07.662
right around the time
I got my first TV show,

00:28:07.662 --> 00:28:10.431
right around the time I
became a titan-in-training,

00:28:10.431 --> 00:28:11.905
right around the time I started competing

00:28:11.905 --> 00:28:14.916
with myself for ways unknown.

00:28:14.916 --> 00:28:17.677
15 minutes, what could be
wrong with giving myself

00:28:17.677 --> 00:28:20.234
my full attention for 15 minutes?

00:28:20.234 --> 00:28:21.817
Turns out, nothing.

00:28:22.666 --> 00:28:23.866
The very act of not working

00:28:23.866 --> 00:28:26.691
has made it possible
for the hum to return,

00:28:26.691 --> 00:28:31.359
as if the hum's engine could
only refuel while I was away.

00:28:31.359 --> 00:28:33.942
Work doesn't work without play.

00:28:35.155 --> 00:28:38.297
It takes a little time,
but after a few months,

00:28:38.297 --> 00:28:41.078
one day the floodgates
open and there's a rush,

00:28:41.078 --> 00:28:43.228
and I find myself standing in my office

00:28:43.228 --> 00:28:45.852
filled with an unfamiliar melody,

00:28:45.852 --> 00:28:48.150
full on groove inside me and around me,

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:50.128
and it sends me spinning with ideas,

00:28:50.128 --> 00:28:51.541
and the humming road is open,

00:28:51.541 --> 00:28:55.730
and I can drive it and drive
it, and I love working again.

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:59.435
But now I like that hum,
but I don't love that hum.

00:28:59.435 --> 00:29:00.645
I don't need that hum.

00:29:00.645 --> 00:29:01.785
I am not that hum.

00:29:01.785 --> 00:29:04.452
That hum is not me, not anymore.

00:29:05.774 --> 00:29:09.405
I am bubbles and sticky fingers
and dinners with friends.

00:29:09.405 --> 00:29:11.077
I am that hum.

00:29:11.077 --> 00:29:12.394
Life's hum.

00:29:12.394 --> 00:29:13.718
Love's hum.

00:29:13.718 --> 00:29:15.789
Work's hum is still a piece of me,

00:29:15.789 --> 00:29:19.956
it is just no longer all of
me, and I am so grateful.

00:29:20.868 --> 00:29:22.640
And I don't give a crap
about being a titan,

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:24.329
because I have never once seen a titan

00:29:24.329 --> 00:29:26.043
play Red Rover, Red Rover.

00:29:26.043 --> 00:29:27.618
(audience laughs)

00:29:27.618 --> 00:29:29.660
I said yes to less work and more play,

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.859
and somehow I still run my world.

00:29:31.859 --> 00:29:33.098
My brain is still global.

00:29:33.098 --> 00:29:35.668
My campfires still burn.

00:29:35.668 --> 00:29:37.514
The more I play, the happier I am,

00:29:37.514 --> 00:29:39.604
and the happier my kids are.

00:29:39.604 --> 00:29:42.971
The more I play, the more
I feel like a good mother.

00:29:42.971 --> 00:29:45.293
The more I play, the
freer my mind becomes.

00:29:45.293 --> 00:29:47.255
The more I play, the better I work.

00:29:47.255 --> 00:29:49.780
The more I play, the more I feel the hum,

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:51.741
the nation I'm building,
the marathon I'm running,

00:29:51.741 --> 00:29:54.726
the troops, the canvas, the
high note, the hum, the hum,

00:29:54.726 --> 00:29:58.059
the other hum, the real hum, life's hum.

00:29:59.239 --> 00:30:01.950
The more I feel that hum,
the more this strange,

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:05.333
quivering, uncocooned, awkward, brand new,

00:30:05.333 --> 00:30:07.833
alive non-titan feels like me.

00:30:08.829 --> 00:30:12.324
The more I feel that hum,
the more I know who I am.

00:30:12.324 --> 00:30:16.169
I'm a writer, I make stuff up, I imagine.

00:30:16.169 --> 00:30:18.967
That part of the job,
that's living the dream.

00:30:18.967 --> 00:30:21.726
That's the dream of the job.

00:30:21.726 --> 00:30:25.568
Because a dream job should
be a little bit dreamy.

00:30:25.568 --> 00:30:29.411
I said yes to less work and more play.

00:30:29.411 --> 00:30:31.904
Titans need not apply.

00:30:31.904 --> 00:30:32.821
Want to play?

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:34.533
Thank you.

00:30:34.533 --> 00:30:37.533
(audience applauds)

00:30:44.559 --> 00:30:46.106
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:30:46.106 --> 00:30:49.108
I made this video because
Tahera Barney asked me to.

00:30:49.108 --> 00:30:50.206
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:30:50.206 --> 00:30:52.049
that you would like me to profile next,

00:30:52.049 --> 00:30:53.552
please leave it down in the comments below

00:30:53.552 --> 00:30:54.552
and I'll see what I can do.

00:30:54.552 --> 00:30:56.294
I'd also love to know, what did Shonda say

00:30:56.294 --> 00:30:58.237
that really had a big impact on you?

00:30:58.237 --> 00:30:59.497
What was your favorite clip?

00:30:59.497 --> 00:31:01.407
What lesson did you learn and why?

00:31:01.407 --> 00:31:03.087
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:31:03.087 --> 00:31:04.730
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:06.319
Thank you again for watching.

00:31:06.319 --> 00:31:07.503
I believe in you.

00:31:07.503 --> 00:31:10.096
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:31:10.096 --> 00:31:12.373
and whatever your one word is.

00:31:12.373 --> 00:31:14.873
Much love, I'll see you soon.

